From 63b5e9ae4831916f5735cad68bed73f5d153a13f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 09:03:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(gateway): hand an externally-supervised gateway back
 before a file-only service restart [daveey]

The restart verb dispatched to the installed service whenever the canonical
plist/unit FILE existed (`_installed_service_kind_for`), before asking who supervises
the live gateway. With a custom launchd agent / unit running
`gateway run --external-supervisor` beside a stale or unloaded canonical definition,
the service call failed and `_cmd_restart` printed "Gateway service restart failed"
and exited 1: the external-supervisor hand-back further down was unreachable.

Reproduced (tests/hermes_cli/test_gateway_restart_installed_service_external_supervisor.py):
a real child whose argv carries `gateway run --external-supervisor`, an installed
launchd stub whose label is not loaded, a failing `_service_call`, and
`restart --detach` parsed by the real gateway parser -> SystemExit(1) before this
change; after it, a real SIGUSR1 drains the child (exit 75), the stubbed supervisor
relaunches it, and the CLI reports the fresh PID.

Now, after the unchanged refusal guards and orphan reap, a gateway that declares an
external supervisor and whose installed launchd/systemd job is not it (or its
launcher ancestor) is handed back via `restart_externally_supervised_gateway` first.
A service that provably owns the gateway (job pid == gateway or ancestor) keeps the
service path (plist refresh + kickstart / systemctl), and its failure is still exit 1.
An unreadable job is not proof of ownership. Windows keeps the service path.

Not proven: that this dispatch order is why the historical Desktop stale-owner
restart exited 1 on David's Macs. Desktop logged only the last combined output line,
so the real stdout/stderr of that run is lost; the companion Desktop commit keeps
both streams for the next occurrence.
